            {
                "entry_id": "90557774-a495-3471-96e8-f5cc0eae2129",
                "word": "caïnisme",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "c01870bd-f768-4ed5-b3d2-5249b3385f12",
                        "entry_id": "90557774-a495-3471-96e8-f5cc0eae2129",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "caïnismes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "c3477329-7ae3-3fd9-99e2-637d63df5d78",
                        "entry_id": "90557774-a495-3471-96e8-f5cc0eae2129",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Pulsion qui pousse à tuer un autre humain, considéré comme frère."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ed667111-2419-4ab1-8f5a-da6b7d2d9cf6",
                                "sense_id": "c3477329-7ae3-3fd9-99e2-637d63df5d78",
                                "text": "Le caïnisme [serait-il] naturel aux frères humains ?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "53e207aa-f92d-4e90-8acc-5dc36f34eacf",
                                "sense_id": "c3477329-7ae3-3fd9-99e2-637d63df5d78",
                                "text": "Cette maladie, — ce vice originel, — est le grand moteur des âmes basses et des intelligences douteuses, il n’a pas de nom ; on pourrait l’appeler le caïnisme. Il a fait les massacres de septembre, il a fait la loi de prairial, il a fait la commune."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "97f5bbfe-bd00-414d-b5d8-85f9c5f52e87",
                                "sense_id": "c3477329-7ae3-3fd9-99e2-637d63df5d78",
                                "text": "Je veux parler de ce qu’il faut bien appeler \"le caïnisme\", c’est à dire cette pulsion irrésistible qui conduit tantôt un pilote a fracasser son avion et ses passagers sur une montagne, tantôt un tunisien fou-furieux à jeter un camion sur la foule qui regarde un feu d’artifices, tantôt un jeune forcené qui ouvre le feu sur les clients d’un centre commercial."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b20d4712-9300-3bd6-992e-d02ace2e2ebb",
                        "entry_id": "90557774-a495-3471-96e8-f5cc0eae2129",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Comportement agressif des nouveau-nés d’une portée les uns envers les autres qui conduit à la mort du plus faible d'une fratrie."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "88a6aa19-6902-4f5c-953c-8d6afd49b862",
                        "source_entry_id": "90557774-a495-3471-96e8-f5cc0eae2129",
                        "target_lang_word": "Kainismus",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": []
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "32df92be-de37-4214-92bd-83c21d49290a",
                        "source_entry_id": "90557774-a495-3471-96e8-f5cc0eae2129",
                        "target_lang_word": "siblicide",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "siblicide",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "46b2a277-3c14-3bc9-b2c0-002deace6d6c",
                                        "entry_id": "ef5e48f4-b6d7-3842-9267-d26ec122c007",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The killing of a sibling, especially as observed among certain species of animals."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5c957500-401b-472c-bba4-271a2ac593e3",
                                                "sense_id": "46b2a277-3c14-3bc9-b2c0-002deace6d6c",
                                                "text": "I continue to question the interpretation of \"fatal sibling aggression\" and active brood reduction as a means of maximizing productivity[…]. To me, the data clearly indicate a simple response to food availability, with the oldest young passively out-competing its smaller sibling for a limited resource. I still have not seen data to support \"siblicide\" as a strategy."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "dff7cbb8-86d7-4a32-aa66-0369420247a5",
                                                "sense_id": "46b2a277-3c14-3bc9-b2c0-002deace6d6c",
                                                "text": "Oophagy and adelphophagy, which are forms of embryonic cannibalism, occur in both cartilaginous and bony fishes. They are variations on the tactic of siblicide (Mock, 1984) as a reproductive strategy. In oophagy, embryos feed upon sibling eggs. […] Adelphophagy, literally eating one's brother, is also known in sharks as intrauterine embryonic cannibalism[…]."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f3b10497-930e-4bed-9a32-326956290f7e",
                                                "sense_id": "46b2a277-3c14-3bc9-b2c0-002deace6d6c",
                                                "text": "Obligate siblicide is best known in various eagles, boobies, and pelicans. All lay two eggs, hatching is asynchronus, and usually the elder sib kills its younger sib soon after hatch. From the parent's perspective, the second egg/chick serves as insurance against the unexpected failure of the first[…]. Interestingly, obligate siblicide seems to be independent of prevailing food at the time of the junior sibling[']s demise."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "186f0106-c24f-4a91-9494-3e7ae247a453",
                                                "sense_id": "46b2a277-3c14-3bc9-b2c0-002deace6d6c",
                                                "text": "If siblicides are victim-based, a mother clearly cannot improve her fitness by producing fewer eggs. Less obviously, mothers may eventually be able to gain by laying more, until eventually they are constrained by the costs of eggs."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "59587f9a-a433-4c9c-847d-bc3a4bd3eb6e",
                                                "sense_id": "46b2a277-3c14-3bc9-b2c0-002deace6d6c",
                                                "text": "One of the more recent examinations of siblicide cases is [John M.] Dawson and [Patrick A.] Langan's Special Report on Murder in Families, published through the Bureau of Justice Statistics (1994). […] According to their findings, 85% of siblicide offenders and 73% of siblicide victims were male. […] Alcohol was reported to play a role in approximately one half of the siblicides in Dawson and Langan's study."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "758f0baa-767d-4d12-b9e7-094e3857d541",
                                                "sense_id": "46b2a277-3c14-3bc9-b2c0-002deace6d6c",
                                                "text": "Infanticide, siblicide, cannibalism, rape, and lethal combat have been documented in many kinds of animals."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
